# Dashboard composition and card features

A dashboard answers a small set of household questions. Arrange it around those questions before choosing a card shape. A card is a container, a view is a layout, and a feature is a capability-dependent control inside a supported card. They are not interchangeable layers.

<h2 id="layouts">Four native view layouts</h2>

| Native view | Local design contract | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sections | Group related tasks under descriptive headings. Put urgent exceptions before routine summaries. | Preserve native section order, grid sizing and the editor's placement rules. |
| Masonry | Let cards retain their intrinsic height; make each understandable independently. | Do not promise a left-to-right reading sequence from visual columns. Check actual keyboard and reading order. |
| Panel | Give one task the available width, such as a map or floor plan. | Supply a usable text alternative and controls outside precision overlays. |
| Sidebar | Put the main task first and related context second. | Related content must remain useful when the native layout collapses on a narrow screen. |

Grid, horizontal stack and vertical stack are card containers, not additional view types. Avoid nested stacks that shrink labels or make unrelated cards look like one control. Prefer responsive sections when a row of controls cannot fit. Native view visibility hides navigation; it is not an authorisation boundary. Keep account permissions separate.

<h2 id="cards">Card hierarchy and generated dashboards</h2>

Use Heading for a task title, Tile for a concise entity with suitable features, Area for a real configured area, and Shortcut for an identified destination or action. Button cards need an action name; a friendly device name alone does not explain whether activation opens details or changes state. Home summary, toggle-group and generated overview cards must retain their native membership and eligibility rules. Counts must include or explain unavailable members.

A Conditional card controls visibility of its child. Entity filter selects matching entities. Neither can establish that a hidden entity is safe, off, absent or forbidden. Distinguish an intentionally empty result from failed data and retain another route to essential controls. Internal startup, error and empty-state cards keep their actual error context; do not disguise a failed card as an empty household.

